---
import Docs from "~/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";
---

<Docs
  title="Maintenance"
  description="Keep .stack.toml in sync with the project's manifests and config files using stack doctor --reconcile and stack remove --all-orphans."
  eyebrow="Reference"
>
  <h2>The drift problem</h2>
  <p>
    Over time, <code>.stack.toml</code> and the project can fall out of sync.
    Someone drops the Supabase SDK from <code>package.json</code> but forgets to
    run <code>stack remove</code>. Or a service is in the config but nothing in
    the project points to it. <code>stack doctor --reconcile</code> reports this
    drift and <code>stack remove --all-orphans</code> cleans up the local side.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-is-scanned">What counts as "used"</h2>
  <p>
    Both commands run the same source scan from the current directory. It reads
    files at the project root, not import statements under <code>src/</code>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Dependencies in <code>package.json</code>, <code>requirements.txt</code>,
      <code>pyproject.toml</code>, <code>go.mod</code> and <code>Cargo.toml</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Provider config files such as <code>vercel.json</code>,{" "}
      <code>fly.toml</code>, <code>supabase/config.toml</code>,{" "}
      <code>wrangler.toml</code>, <code>firebase.json</code>,{" "}
      <code>render.yaml</code> and <code>.github/workflows/*.yml</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Variable names in <code>.env.example</code>, <code>.env.sample</code> or{" "}
      <code>.env.template</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A service is an <strong>orphan</strong> when it is in{" "}
    <code>.stack.toml</code> and the scan does not find it. It is{" "}
    <strong>untracked</strong> when the scan finds it and{" "}
    <code>.stack.toml</code> does not list it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reconcile"><code>stack doctor --reconcile</code></h2>
  <p>
    Compares the scan with <code>.stack.toml</code>. It is read-only and needs
    no credentials or Phantom.
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`stack doctor --reconcile`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <Code
    code={`  ● 2 services configured · 2 detected in source

  ⚠ 1 orphan: posthog (no longer imported — run \`stack remove posthog\`)
  ○ 1 untracked: clerk (found in package.json — run \`stack add clerk\`)`}
    lang="text"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    With no drift it prints <code>No drift — configured services match
    source.</code> and exits 0. With drift it exits 2. Adding{" "}
    <code>--fix</code> or <code>--all</code> runs the normal health checks
    after the drift report.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>stack doctor --reconcile --json</code> prints the{" "}
    <code>configured</code>, <code>detected</code>, <code>orphans</code> and{" "}
    <code>untracked</code> lists inside the shared doctor envelope, with{" "}
    <code>native_status</code> set to <code>in-sync</code> or{" "}
    <code>drift</code> and one <code>warn</code> check per difference. It skips
    the health checks and exits 0 even on drift, so tooling must read{" "}
    <code>status</code> or the lists.
  </p>

  <h2 id="remove-orphans"><code>stack remove --all-orphans</code></h2>
  <p>
    Runs the scan again (it does not reuse a saved{" "}
    <code>doctor --reconcile</code> result), lists the orphans with their
    secret counts, then removes them from <code>.stack.toml</code>, removes
    their MCP entries and attempts to remove their Phantom secrets. It keeps
    upstream resources. In a terminal it asks for confirmation first. Without a
    TTY (CI, agent shells) it does not ask and removes the orphans at once.
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`# Preview first
stack doctor --reconcile

# Then remove orphans
stack remove --all-orphans`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <div class="callout callout-warn">
    <code>stack remove --all-orphans</code> does not delete upstream projects.
    Inspect the orphan list before removing local configuration, especially
    from an agent shell, where there is no confirmation prompt. Phantom secret
    removal requires its terminal confirmation; headless cleanup or refused
    confirmation can leave secrets stored. Inspect any reported leftovers.
    <code>--keepRemote</code> is a compatibility flag, not a switch for remote deletion.
  </div>

  <h2>Using both in CI</h2>
  <p>
    To fail a job on drift, run <code>stack doctor --reconcile</code> without{" "}
    <code>--json</code> and let exit code 2 fail the step. To collect a report
    instead, save <code>stack doctor --reconcile --json</code> and inspect its
    fields. See{" "}
    <a href="/docs/ci/#json-report-shape">CI integration → source-drift report</a>{" "}
    and <a href="/docs/ci/#scan-in-ci"><code>stack scan</code> in CI</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
